# DTF tier manifest: range-serves-1120
#   (artifact-keeper#5145, #5478, #5502, #5509, #5668 (Range part), #5139;
#   all fixed on main for 1.12.0, none on the 1.11.x line)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a run-time-filled upstream (upstreams.mock-files:
# a stdlib-python server on 172.16.<slot>.76 whose files the oracle writes over
# a control API and which counts every GET per path). It stands in for the
# Hugging Face Hub, an Alpine mirror and a generic upstream.
#
# THE BUGS
# #5145  The Hugging Face `resolve` route ignored Range: 200 + the whole file,
#        no Accept-Ranges, for a hosted file and for a cached Remote file, so
#        an interrupted `hf download` (or `curl -C -`) could never resume.
# #5478  `GET /alpine/{repo}/{branch}/{repository}/{arch}/{file}.apk` ignored
#        Range (rules_apko fetches an .apk's three gzip sections as ranged
#        GETs and checks each against a lockfile digest).
# #5502  The generic download route ignored Range on a Remote's proxied serves
#        (a cache hit or an upstream fetch); only stored artifacts were ranged.
# #5509  A ranged GET on a cold alpine Remote answered 206 but the tee into
#        the proxy cache was dropped once the window was written, so the
#        cache never filled and every repeat went upstream.
# #5668  The native Maven route `GET /maven/{repo}/...` ignored Range (200,
#        full body, no Accept-Ranges), so segmented downloads were corrupted.
#        Only the Range part of #5668 is gated here.
# #5139  Accepted connections never set TCP_NODELAY. A small body (below one
#        MSS) is written after the response head, Nagle holds it until the
#        head is ACKed and the receiver delays that ACK by ~40 ms, so every
#        small download on a keep-alive connection took ~44 ms.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      RED on ak 1.11.13 (none of the fixes is on the 1.11.x line),
#              GREEN on main:
#              #5145 hosted and cached-Remote HF resolve: Range -> 206 with
#                    Content-Range and the exact window, 416 past the end, and
#                    `curl -C -` resumes a partial download to the exact file;
#              #5478 hosted .apk: Range -> 206 + Accept-Ranges, 416 past end;
#              #5502 generic Remote: Range on a cache hit and on a cold fetch
#                    -> 206 with the exact window, 416 past the end;
#              #5509 cold alpine Remote, Range ending before EOF -> 206, then
#                    the cache fills: a later whole GET is served without a
#                    second upstream GET;
#              #5668 hosted Maven jar: Range -> 206 + Accept-Ranges, 416;
#              #5139 the median time of 40 GETs of a 1000-byte Maven file on
#                    one keep-alive connection, measured from a container on
#                    the backend's network, is under 20 ms (pre-fix ~44 ms;
#                    42 ms measured on 1.11.13).
#   (CONTROL)  must hold on BOTH images: whole GETs on every route serve the
#              exact bytes; a 300 kB Maven file on the same kind of
#              connection is under 20 ms. It spans several segments, so Nagle
#              never waits on a delayed ACK for it, and a slow host fails the
#              control rather than the fix. (A 16 kB file is not a control:
#              the rootless-podman bridge MTU is 65520, so it is one segment.)
#
# TIMING (#5139): the pre-fix median is the kernel's delayed-ACK floor (40 ms
# minimum on Linux) plus a little; the fixed median is ~1 ms. The 20 ms bound
# leaves a wide margin on both sides, and the 300 kB control shows that the
# host itself is fast enough when the bound is missed.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~120 requests back to back.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the mock upstream is on a private subnet.
COVERS="5145 5478 5502 5509 5668 5139"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-files"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
